      <div class="Section3"><a name="_cultural_naming_profiles" id="_cultural_naming_profiles"></a>
        <h1 class="Annex"><b>Annex B</b><br/>(informative)<br/><br/><b>Cultural naming profiles</b></h1>
        <div><a name="_afghan" id="_afghan"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.1 Afghan</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_albanian" id="_albanian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.2 Albanian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_arab_muslim" id="_arab_muslim"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.3 Arab-Muslim</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_bangladeshi" id="_bangladeshi"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.4 Bangladeshi</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_bosnian_serbian_croats" id="_bosnian_serbian_croats"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.5 Bosnian, Serbian, Croats</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_brazilian" id="_brazilian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.6 Brazilian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_bulgarian" id="_bulgarian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.7 Bulgarian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_chinese" id="_chinese"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.8 Chinese</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a72b083a-3073-4130-b20b-87825daccfcc" id="_a72b083a-3073-4130-b20b-87825daccfcc"></a>family name + given name(s)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_d44196ce-e445-4f03-9f77-efc7451b7ae9" id="_d44196ce-e445-4f03-9f77-efc7451b7ae9"></a>Chinese personal names are names used by those from mainland China, Hong Kong,
Macau, Taiwan, and the Chinese diaspora overseas.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_0d44f849-e6c7-4f48-8081-32fc2dc6f842" id="_0d44f849-e6c7-4f48-8081-32fc2dc6f842"></a>Chinese names consist of a family name (&#x59D3;, x&#xEC;ng), followed by
a given name (&#x540D;, m&#xED;ng).</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_6fdd4829-0c66-408c-a728-5fc830bdc623" id="_6fdd4829-0c66-408c-a728-5fc830bdc623"></a>Some people also utilize a &#x201C;courtesy name&#x201D; or &#x201C;style name&#x201D; (&#x5B57;, z&#xEC;),
by which they were known among those outside their
family and closest friends.</p></div>
        <div><a name="_colombian" id="_colombian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.9 Colombian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_czech" id="_czech"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.10 Czech</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_english" id="_english"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.11 English</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_01ae7129-6e02-4547-b286-0bea43e95b82" id="_01ae7129-6e02-4547-b286-0bea43e95b82"></a>given name(s) (+ optional family name)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_140cb93b-9fe5-41f7-92bc-06c7a0e2bb88" id="_140cb93b-9fe5-41f7-92bc-06c7a0e2bb88"></a>English names are names used in, or originating in, England. In England as
elsewhere in the English-speaking world, a complete name usually consists of a
given name, commonly referred to as a first name or Christian name, and a (most
commonly patrilineal) family name or surname, also referred to as a last name.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_08c8ab9f-2ccd-4658-8bf2-23369c6c7720" id="_08c8ab9f-2ccd-4658-8bf2-23369c6c7720"></a>There can be several given names, some of these being often referred to as a
second name, or middle name(s).</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a873b6f5-d8a9-490b-93d2-ae5cb873adc7" id="_a873b6f5-d8a9-490b-93d2-ae5cb873adc7"></a>Double-barrelled names, compound surnames in English feature two words,
are often joined by a hyphen or hyphens.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_9c4ab0ec-ace8-4197-9751-c3020ad3273c" id="_9c4ab0ec-ace8-4197-9751-c3020ad3273c"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Henry Hepburne-Scott</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_49320e24-a3b4-4d68-999c-0ecfcb86ac79" id="_49320e24-a3b4-4d68-999c-0ecfcb86ac79"></a>Some families having as many as
three or four words making up their surname.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_3df53623-1625-4cc3-925b-b1f7b5c307b4" id="_3df53623-1625-4cc3-925b-b1f7b5c307b4"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Charles Hepburn-Stuart-Forbes-Trefusis, 21st Baron Clinton.</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_e016e1d5-1661-47d9-91d2-c07394492f83" id="_e016e1d5-1661-47d9-91d2-c07394492f83"></a>It is not unusual for compound surnames to be composed of separate words
not linked by a hyphen.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_6cd7ab66-3cfc-4e29-a313-ec65a0058a7c" id="_6cd7ab66-3cfc-4e29-a313-ec65a0058a7c"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Iain Duncan Smith has a surname of &#x201C;Duncan Smith&#x201D;.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_french" id="_french"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.12 French</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_ee31de24-8c81-43d0-8a4c-954acff1a4d9" id="_ee31de24-8c81-43d0-8a4c-954acff1a4d9"></a>given name(s) (+ optional family name)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_e52739fd-f2a0-4878-adc6-a18e7f544ebf" id="_e52739fd-f2a0-4878-adc6-a18e7f544ebf"></a>French people have one, two or more given names (first names). One of them
is used in daily life.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_883a1338-99e1-426b-b631-085902c8afb9" id="_883a1338-99e1-426b-b631-085902c8afb9"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Jacques Chirac&#x201D; instead of officially of Jacques Ren&#xE9; Chirac.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_ab0acbf0-2988-48e8-9b1c-dac819d0f680" id="_ab0acbf0-2988-48e8-9b1c-dac819d0f680"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Philippe P&#xE9;tain&#x201D; instead of officially of Henri Philippe P&#xE9;tain,
because Philippe was the given name that he used in daily
life.</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_c324f52f-0414-4f01-98ba-9d3b541d69b7" id="_c324f52f-0414-4f01-98ba-9d3b541d69b7"></a>Middle names in the English sense do not exist, and middle initials are
never used for second or further given names.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_2489ad0c-6e2d-4c13-8648-95ed98c7cc50" id="_2489ad0c-6e2d-4c13-8648-95ed98c7cc50"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Claude All&#xE8;gre may be cited as &#x201C;Claude J. All&#xE8;gre&#x201D; in English-speaking scientific publications, but not in French ones.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_german" id="_german"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.13 German</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_7d81bca1-74cb-4406-b35a-058c08bacf7e" id="_7d81bca1-74cb-4406-b35a-058c08bacf7e"></a>given name(s) (+ optional family name)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_d9fd2dfa-cc1c-4f2f-923b-fc1c094b28d4" id="_d9fd2dfa-cc1c-4f2f-923b-fc1c094b28d4"></a>Personal names in German-speaking Europe consist of one or several given names
(Vorname, plural Vornamen) and a surname (Nachname, Familienname). The Vorname
is usually gender-specific.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_ea9e087b-bd35-4151-bc21-f0ca278a9f6a" id="_ea9e087b-bd35-4151-bc21-f0ca278a9f6a"></a>Typical order of &#x201C;given name, surname&#x201D;.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_6615fbfb-9efe-4d9e-9f35-b546a010c7f8" id="_6615fbfb-9efe-4d9e-9f35-b546a010c7f8"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Otto Eduard Leopold F&#xFC;rst von Bismarck.</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_881091b5-b510-4e62-9598-55381dd4e1df" id="_881091b5-b510-4e62-9598-55381dd4e1df"></a>The Vorname (in English forename) is usually given to a child by the parents
shortly after birth. It is common to give a child several Vornamen (forenames).</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_f76ee50c-452d-48de-a963-8abe0985b342" id="_f76ee50c-452d-48de-a963-8abe0985b342"></a>The Rufname (&#x201C;appellation name&#x201D; or &#x201C;call name&#x201D;) is the given name
intended for everyday use, and is often underlined on official documents.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_e2a989ad-c94f-4081-929d-eb94d3cc8eab" id="_e2a989ad-c94f-4081-929d-eb94d3cc8eab"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>The Rufname of the mathematician Amalie Emmy Noether is &#x201C;Emmy&#x201D;.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_greek" id="_greek"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.14 Greek</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_hindu" id="_hindu"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.15 Hindu</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_hungarian" id="_hungarian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.16 Hungarian</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_d876dfe1-a6a2-4d33-9d1b-2177abfa9440" id="_d876dfe1-a6a2-4d33-9d1b-2177abfa9440"></a>family name(s) + given name(s)</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_4e5a416e-f192-4df5-b0d9-4c4756fbfb32" id="_4e5a416e-f192-4df5-b0d9-4c4756fbfb32"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>KOVA&#x301;CS Ja&#x301;nos</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_5b2d2ee9-efce-49ef-9043-4886521553c2" id="_5b2d2ee9-efce-49ef-9043-4886521553c2"></a>If there is more than one given name, they come in order of importance
after the family name:</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_2767618f-40d4-4248-b8de-72be2f5ce47c" id="_2767618f-40d4-4248-b8de-72be2f5ce47c"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>KOVA&#x301;CS Ja&#x301;nos Alada&#x301;r.</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_98e61a7f-0187-44c6-b251-ba880bd07b2d" id="_98e61a7f-0187-44c6-b251-ba880bd07b2d"></a>There can be more than one family name, which can be written fully or abbreviated
into initials:</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_62eea5d9-cd6b-4b40-9dfa-817e1e035f29" id="_62eea5d9-cd6b-4b40-9dfa-817e1e035f29"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>SEGESDY KOVA&#x301;CS Ja&#x301;nos</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_45bd9b2f-edcc-4e93-87c6-3d5540ab1239" id="_45bd9b2f-edcc-4e93-87c6-3d5540ab1239"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  4</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>SEGESDY-KOVA&#x301;CS Ja&#x301;nos</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_6885e26b-cc09-47aa-a348-7c7eb3dd69ad" id="_6885e26b-cc09-47aa-a348-7c7eb3dd69ad"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  5</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>S.KOVA&#x301;CS Ja&#x301;nos</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_77a0155e-343b-4e6c-ad85-4de8ad45a249" id="_77a0155e-343b-4e6c-ad85-4de8ad45a249"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  6</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>SEGESDY K. Ja&#x301;nos</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_icelandic" id="_icelandic"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.17 Icelandic</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a99b8222-ab45-441f-8e3b-a4b14a336097" id="_a99b8222-ab45-441f-8e3b-a4b14a336097"></a>given name(s) + (patronymic or matronymic) (+ optional genonymic) (+ optional family name)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_47c0bf3a-2a57-4944-9789-e6a2a18d9248" id="_47c0bf3a-2a57-4944-9789-e6a2a18d9248"></a>Icelandic names differ from most current Western family name systems by being
patronymic or occasionally matronymic: they indicate the father (or mother) of
the child and not the historic family lineage.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_5bcc5e97-826f-47ec-b3d2-22c4dba7f03e" id="_5bcc5e97-826f-47ec-b3d2-22c4dba7f03e"></a>Iceland&#x2019;s traditional name system was formerly used by all Nordic countries
except partly Finland.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_74c15005-b5ab-4cdc-b2a4-0f46c33fa6dd" id="_74c15005-b5ab-4cdc-b2a4-0f46c33fa6dd"></a>The Icelandic system is not based on family names
(although some people do have family names and might use both systems).
Generally, a person&#x2019;s second name indicates the first name of their father
(patronymic) or in some cases mother (matronymic). According to Icelandic
naming tradition, second names end in -son or -d&#xF3;ttir with few exceptions.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_2aa70280-08d1-440f-b525-98dc9d6075be" id="_2aa70280-08d1-440f-b525-98dc9d6075be"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>J&#xF3;hanna Sigur&#xF0;ard&#xF3;ttir. &#x201C;J&#xF3;hanna, daughter of Sigur&#xF0;ur&#x201D;.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_f9f1cb50-6203-4d0f-98b1-a47e686cdd57" id="_f9f1cb50-6203-4d0f-98b1-a47e686cdd57"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Matronymic. Hei&#xF0;ar Helguson, &#x201C;Helguson, son of Helga&#x201D;.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_814436bb-7b6d-4eb2-8677-7765ab451957" id="_814436bb-7b6d-4eb2-8677-7765ab451957"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#xD6;rvar &#xDE;&#xF3;reyjarson Sm&#xE1;rason. (&#xD6;rvar, son of &#xDE;&#xF3;rey, son of Sm&#xE1;ri)</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_ddf6806f-8e7b-4063-bc29-f12185b45006" id="_ddf6806f-8e7b-4063-bc29-f12185b45006"></a>Some family names do exist in Iceland, most commonly adaptations from last name
patronyms Icelanders took up when living abroad.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_7d971c07-af0f-4838-a323-782b9d9eeab4" id="_7d971c07-af0f-4838-a323-782b9d9eeab4"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  4</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Geir Haarde, Ei&#xF0;ur Sm&#xE1;ri Gu&#xF0;johnsen, Anita Briem.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_21949496-454c-428b-a439-619ba467bbab" id="_21949496-454c-428b-a439-619ba467bbab"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  5</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Nobel Prize-winning author Halld&#xF3;r Laxness, born as Halld&#xF3;r Gu&#xF0;j&#xF3;nsson.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_indian" id="_indian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.18 Indian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_indonesian" id="_indonesian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.19 Indonesian</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_ba370fff-38f3-49a6-8fd0-8dc513ebf357" id="_ba370fff-38f3-49a6-8fd0-8dc513ebf357"></a>given name(s)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_26a6dc25-7f8b-4a46-942c-bbacbe98afb5" id="_26a6dc25-7f8b-4a46-942c-bbacbe98afb5"></a>Indonesians generally use given names without a family name.
The majority of Indonesians do not have family names. Rather,
their given names are geographically and culturally specific.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_4f9b1988-731a-4aac-a9c8-b476484e4a45" id="_4f9b1988-731a-4aac-a9c8-b476484e4a45"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Mulyono, the original name of Joko Widodo.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_bf5c9165-9913-42b7-8b9c-176e3ca9af42" id="_bf5c9165-9913-42b7-8b9c-176e3ca9af42"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Kusno Sosrodihardjo.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_e1932b6e-bd56-4fe5-9260-6c993ea640b2" id="_e1932b6e-bd56-4fe5-9260-6c993ea640b2"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Fransiskus Xaverius Hadi Rudyatmo</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_2d046894-ebc0-46ec-9b17-03fcff533544" id="_2d046894-ebc0-46ec-9b17-03fcff533544"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  4</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Sudono Salim</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_6840c7e9-1d4a-4798-9b2c-d8df4a0ea8c3" id="_6840c7e9-1d4a-4798-9b2c-d8df4a0ea8c3"></a>Some given names include a family name or a patronymic.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_75e73d64-e6e6-45c3-a99e-26c69a23ef96" id="_75e73d64-e6e6-45c3-a99e-26c69a23ef96"></a>Indonesian names usually fall into one of the following categories:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo19;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    Mononymic names
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo19;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Polynymic names without a family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo19;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Polynymic names with a family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo19;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    Polynymic names with patronymic
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_71c7b077-cb8f-429a-9e8e-ae8abd7d111e" id="_71c7b077-cb8f-429a-9e8e-ae8abd7d111e"></a>An Indonesian name is often mis-represented in other cultures, such as:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo20;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    Netherlands: the word &#x201C;Onbekend&#x201D; (&#x201C;unknown&#x201D;) is assigned as a last name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo20;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Germany: the one-word name is used as both given name and surname
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo20;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    US: &#x201C;Fnu&#x201D; (family name unknown) is assigned as the first or given name, &#x201C;Lnu&#x201D; (last name unknown) is assigned as the family, surname or last name,
or the name is duplicated as the surname.
  </p>
</div>
        <div><a name="_iranian" id="_iranian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.20 Iranian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_iraqi" id="_iraqi"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.21 Iraqi</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_italian" id="_italian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.22 Italian</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_05f8939c-4cdf-4b63-ae8e-90059e89b291" id="_05f8939c-4cdf-4b63-ae8e-90059e89b291"></a>given name(s) + family name</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_8a001cf4-2a5b-4291-b71e-2e23750011e6" id="_8a001cf4-2a5b-4291-b71e-2e23750011e6"></a>A name in Italian consists of a given name (nome) and a surname (cognome).
Surnames are normally written after given names.</p></div>
        <div><a name="_japanese" id="_japanese"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.23 Japanese</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_085cb95d-205c-4a6b-b763-e89095cb283f" id="_085cb95d-205c-4a6b-b763-e89095cb283f"></a>family name + given name(s)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_10fc389a-a88b-4f6c-8944-bb56b3d7e3fb" id="_10fc389a-a88b-4f6c-8944-bb56b3d7e3fb"></a>Japanese names usually consist of a family name (surname),
followed by a given name.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_22a03147-4857-4cea-a80f-c933379f036f" id="_22a03147-4857-4cea-a80f-c933379f036f"></a>Japanese names are usually written in kanji,
where characters are logographic, but each with a variety of
possible Japanese pronunciations. Therefore
often names cannot in general be spelled or
pronounced unless both the spelling and pronunciation are given.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_e215ae45-2e99-4692-8b5b-5bb81610102d" id="_e215ae45-2e99-4692-8b5b-5bb81610102d"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x5927;&#x7FD4; is traditionally pronounced &#x201C;Hiroto&#x201D;, but can also be pronounced as
&#x201C;Haruto&#x201D;, &#x201C;Yamato&#x201D;, &#x201C;Taiga&#x201D;, &#x201C;Sora&#x201D;, &#x201C;Taito&#x201D;, &#x201C;Daito&#x201D;, and
&#x201C;Masato&#x201D;.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_2d6ae065-8b5f-4286-a9bf-8d77c37e2fd1" id="_2d6ae065-8b5f-4286-a9bf-8d77c37e2fd1"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x5927;&#x548C; can be read as Daiwa or Yamato.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_40bc9e85-c25b-4598-9417-5a45486667eb" id="_40bc9e85-c25b-4598-9417-5a45486667eb"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x6771;&#x6D77;&#x6797; can be read as T&#x14D;kairin or Sh&#x14D;ji</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_jewish" id="_jewish"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.24 Jewish</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_d5c4eaa6-8d73-46d1-aaa1-6e754f62f51e" id="_d5c4eaa6-8d73-46d1-aaa1-6e754f62f51e"></a>given name(s) + family names</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_b40dcabb-d434-4c45-91fe-8d3607c99838" id="_b40dcabb-d434-4c45-91fe-8d3607c99838"></a>or</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_b562f8f2-75e8-4626-bf83-4a8891f93c65" id="_b562f8f2-75e8-4626-bf83-4a8891f93c65"></a>given name(s) + patronymic name</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_f3235de6-8af3-44d1-9257-95e4975c64ad" id="_f3235de6-8af3-44d1-9257-95e4975c64ad"></a>Modern Jewish names include Hebrew patronymic names as well as family names.</p></div>
        <div><a name="_korean" id="_korean"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.25 Korean</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_7ecde3c9-3f17-46a1-b18d-c3cc8edb1c9c" id="_7ecde3c9-3f17-46a1-b18d-c3cc8edb1c9c"></a>family name + given name(s)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_52b1d4b8-2b2e-46d1-90a3-44693e2e78bb" id="_52b1d4b8-2b2e-46d1-90a3-44693e2e78bb"></a>A Korean name consists of a family name followed by a given name, as used by
the Korean people in both South Korea and North Korea.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_52977689-1d38-4f9f-8c91-cf483dc8859f" id="_52977689-1d38-4f9f-8c91-cf483dc8859f"></a>In the Korean language,
ireum or seongmyeong usually refers to the family name (seong) and given name
(myeong) together.</p></div>
        <div><a name="_kurdish" id="_kurdish"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.26 Kurdish</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_latvian" id="_latvian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.27 Latvian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_lithuanian" id="_lithuanian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.28 Lithuanian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_macedonian" id="_macedonian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.29 Macedonian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_manchu" id="_manchu"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.30 Manchu</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_2cb1744f-7ab1-49c7-8687-a707318a24db" id="_2cb1744f-7ab1-49c7-8687-a707318a24db"></a>given name(s) (+ optional family name)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_09ad724e-c6ee-4536-b365-9d61a8a75cec" id="_09ad724e-c6ee-4536-b365-9d61a8a75cec"></a>Manchus are called only by their given names.
Manchu clan names (&#x201C;hala&#x201D;) are not used with their personal names,
but occasionally a family name is used.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_bc76b1fd-cfea-49c0-a716-4ef6d6aecf6e" id="_bc76b1fd-cfea-49c0-a716-4ef6d6aecf6e"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Tuli&#x161;en</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_a7b27141-797a-45b4-8ce9-15ab13406ebd" id="_a7b27141-797a-45b4-8ce9-15ab13406ebd"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Hiowan Ye (&#x7384;&#x70E8;)</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_e7a114d4-9823-4bee-992c-8ca19cbb5f61" id="_e7a114d4-9823-4bee-992c-8ca19cbb5f61"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Aisin-Gioro Ulhicun, linguist. The family name is &#x201C;Aisin-Gioro&#x201D;.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_mongolian" id="_mongolian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.31 Mongolian</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_71e1ebdd-65c4-4771-9024-b557821d1fe7" id="_71e1ebdd-65c4-4771-9024-b557821d1fe7"></a>given name (+ optional clan name or family name)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_fc48c90e-78a2-4ca5-953a-313d2173bae0" id="_fc48c90e-78a2-4ca5-953a-313d2173bae0"></a>or</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_8e9c11d6-41c6-4af1-b4ae-21d9964c5de6" id="_8e9c11d6-41c6-4af1-b4ae-21d9964c5de6"></a>genonymic + given name</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_539b4c83-c71c-4992-afc4-dabc121b09b9" id="_539b4c83-c71c-4992-afc4-dabc121b09b9"></a>Mongolian names traditionally consists of only one personal name.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_725bf8d5-96dc-4431-bb7d-f3b549a34b9e" id="_725bf8d5-96dc-4431-bb7d-f3b549a34b9e"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x41E;&#x442;&#x440;&#x44F;&#x434;&#x44B;&#x43D; &#x413;&#x4AF;&#x43D;&#x434;&#x44D;&#x433;&#x43C;&#x430;&#x430; (&#x201C;Otryadyn G&#xFC;ndegmaa&#x201D;), a Mongolian shooter, has &#x201C;Otryadyn&#x201D; as a patronym, &#x201C;G&#xFC;ndegmaa&#x201D; as given name.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_ea780fb3-c347-40ca-ab81-5337df91f004" id="_ea780fb3-c347-40ca-ab81-5337df91f004"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x425;&#x430;&#x43B;&#x442;&#x43C;&#x430;&#x430;&#x433;&#x438;&#x439;&#x43D; &#x411;&#x430;&#x442;&#x442;&#x443;&#x43B;&#x433;&#x430; (&#x201C;Khaltmaagiin Battulga&#x201D;), President of Mongolia.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_nigerian" id="_nigerian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.32 Nigerian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_pakistani" id="_pakistani"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.33 Pakistani</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_portuguese" id="_portuguese"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.34 Portuguese</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_19545470-d421-40d7-8d4a-72f5e04b0b3b" id="_19545470-d421-40d7-8d4a-72f5e04b0b3b"></a>given name(s) + mother&#x2019;s patronymic name(s) + father&#x2019;s patronymic name(s)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_b25fa279-58be-43d1-bae9-49f5db78b271" id="_b25fa279-58be-43d1-bae9-49f5db78b271"></a>Portuguese often have two personal names, although the first may at times be used on its own.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_3ec24742-fc30-4e27-9a75-d743805d04e6" id="_3ec24742-fc30-4e27-9a75-d743805d04e6"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>George Agostinho Baptista da Silva.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_163589f0-aac0-407f-b0c5-e3959e8d60c8" id="_163589f0-aac0-407f-b0c5-e3959e8d60c8"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Ant&#xF3;nio Caetano de Abreu Freire Egas Moniz, Nobel prize winner. He was the child of Fernando de Pina Rezende Abreu and Maria do Rosario de Almeida e Sousa.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_romanian" id="_romanian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.35 Romanian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_russian" id="_russian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.36 Russian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_sikh" id="_sikh"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.37 Sikh</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_sinhalese" id="_sinhalese"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.38 Sinhalese</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_slavic" id="_slavic"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.39 Slavic</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_fd61d1fe-5042-4f10-98d0-b72a0a0751d1" id="_fd61d1fe-5042-4f10-98d0-b72a0a0751d1"></a>given name + patronymic name + family name</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_2a1a8f26-6121-494c-bce1-75d0b089c3f8" id="_2a1a8f26-6121-494c-bce1-75d0b089c3f8"></a>Slavic naming applies to Russian, Ukrainian, Belorussian,
Bulgaria, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Serbia and Macedonia,
as well as some populations of Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Uzbekistan and Turkmenistan.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_2ed09fd0-8bba-4cae-99ee-4aea1eebda7e" id="_2ed09fd0-8bba-4cae-99ee-4aea1eebda7e"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x418;&#x432;&#x430;&#x301;&#x43D; &#x41A;&#x43E;&#x43D;&#x441;&#x442;&#x430;&#x43D;&#x442;&#x438;&#x301;&#x43D;&#x43E;&#x432;&#x438;&#x447; &#x410;&#x439;&#x432;&#x430;&#x437;&#x43E;&#x301;&#x432;&#x441;&#x43A;&#x438;&#x439; (&#x201C;Ivan Konstantinovich Aivazovsky&#x201D;). Ivan is the given name, Konstantinovich the patronymic name, Aivazovsky the family name.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_b72d67ea-e248-473c-9f11-acd9a938690f" id="_b72d67ea-e248-473c-9f11-acd9a938690f"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x41E;&#x43B;&#x435;&#x43A;&#x441;&#x430;&#x43D;&#x434;&#x440; &#x406;&#x432;&#x430;&#x43D;&#x43E;&#x432;&#x438;&#x447; &#x411;&#x456;&#x43B;&#x430;&#x448;, (&#x201C;Oleksandr Ivanovych Bilash&#x201D;).  Oleksandr is the given name, Ivanovych the patronymic name, Bilash the family name.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_slovenian" id="_slovenian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.40 Slovenian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_somali" id="_somali"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.41 Somali</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_spanish" id="_spanish"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.42 Spanish</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_331cb86c-9599-4732-9130-40f7d4f7facd" id="_331cb86c-9599-4732-9130-40f7d4f7facd"></a>given name(s) + father&#x2019;s patronymic name(s) + mother&#x2019;s patronymic name(s)</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_8aea9239-58d1-45b9-800d-e98040ffa350" id="_8aea9239-58d1-45b9-800d-e98040ffa350"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Salvador de Madariaga y Rojo, Spanish diplomat, writer, historian and pacifist. Child of Dar&#xED;o Jos&#xE9; de Madariaga&#x200B; and Mar&#xED;a Ascensi&#xF3;n Rojo.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_tamil" id="_tamil"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.43 Tamil</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_thai" id="_thai"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.44 Thai</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_67351e1c-58ff-487e-ae4b-bc25e3401489" id="_67351e1c-58ff-487e-ae4b-bc25e3401489"></a>given name(s) + family name</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_c63b09b6-fb48-40e0-b294-54387da67a59" id="_c63b09b6-fb48-40e0-b294-54387da67a59"></a>Modern Thai names are composed of given names followed by the family name.
Prior to 1913, family names were not required.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_f3b9587b-ecb3-4110-a1b0-f3b42c6b9c3d" id="_f3b9587b-ecb3-4110-a1b0-f3b42c6b9c3d"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#xE0A;&#xE27;&#xE19; &#xE2B;&#xE25;&#xE35;&#xE01;&#xE20;&#xE31;&#xE22; (&#x201C;Chuan Likphai&#x201D;)</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_turkish" id="_turkish"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.45 Turkish</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_ukrainian" id="_ukrainian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.46 Ukrainian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_vietnamese" id="_vietnamese"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.47 Vietnamese</p>

</div>
      </div>
      <p class="MsoNormal">
        <br clear="all" style="mso-special-character:line-break;page-break-before:always"/>
      </p>
      <div>
        <h1 class="Section3">Bibliography</h1>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO9945" id="ISO9945"></a>[1]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC/IEEE 9945:2009, <i> Portable Operating System Interface (POSIX&#xAE;) Base Specifications, Issue 7</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO19788-5" id="ISO19788-5"></a>[2]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC 19788-5:2012, <i> Learning, education and training</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO12234-3" id="ISO12234-3"></a>[3]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO 12234-3:2016, <i> Removable memory</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO9075-1" id="ISO9075-1"></a>[4]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC 9075-1:2016, <i> Database languages</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO14813-5" id="ISO14813-5"></a>[5]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO 14813-5:2010, <i> Reference model architecture(s) for the ITS sector</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO14198" id="ISO14198"></a>[6]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/TS 14198:2012, <i> Ergonomic aspects of transport information and control systems</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO29003" id="ISO29003"></a>[7]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC TS 29003:2018, <i> Security techniques</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO14105" id="ISO14105"></a>[8]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/TR 14105:2011, <i> Change management for successful electronic document management system (EDMS) implementation</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO20944-1" id="ISO20944-1"></a>[9]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC 20944-1:2013, <i> Metadata Registries Interoperability and Bindings (MDR-IB)</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO5127" id="ISO5127"></a>[10]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO 5127:2017, <i> Foundation and vocabulary</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO31320" id="ISO31320"></a>[11]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC/IEEE 31320-2:2012, <i> Modeling Languages</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="iso639" id="iso639"></a>[12]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO 639, <i> Code for the representation of names of languages</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO639-3" id="ISO639-3"></a>[13]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO 639-3, <i> Codes for the representation of names of languages</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO15961" id="ISO15961"></a>[14]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC 15961:2004, <i> Radio frequency identification (RFID) for item management</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="iso15924" id="iso15924"></a>[15]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO 15924:2004, <i> Codes for the representation of names of scripts</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="rfc2046" id="rfc2046"></a>[16]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>IETF RFC 2046, <i>Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME) Part Two: Media Types</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="rfc3986" id="rfc3986"></a>[17]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>IETF RFC 3986, <i>Uniform Resource Identifier (URI): Generic Syntax</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="rfc8288" id="rfc8288"></a>[18]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>IETF RFC 8288, <i>Web Linking</i></p>
